From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733: de: commit the datapath through the register command queue

[Recipe cut: the rcq module parameter is not carried: the register command
queue is the one datapath variant.]

Cursor-position writes tore the picture. Every display-engine datapath write took effect the moment the CPU
stored it, wherever the raster was. A cursor-position write that lands
while the blender is on the cursor's own pixels corrupts the rest of the
frame: on the 6 GiB board (2026-09-30) one COORD1 write per frame gives
the two correct sink CRCs at almost every line (the few extra ones matched
the greeter clock's minute tick), and a delay sweep inside the cursor's
rows isolates the slot: 11.3-11.9 us into a line, 7 and 9 distinct frames
for a two-frame sequence; Xorg moves the cursor at 125 Hz with no relation to the raster
and hits that slot often enough to be seen, and a legacy-cursor stimulus
through the DRM API gives 49 (125 Hz) and 170 (1000 Hz) distinct CRCs
where 2 are correct. No
CPU timing fixes it: the slot moves with the cursor, and a write timed to
the vertical blank depends on interrupt latency.

THE QUEUE, AS MEASURED

Allwinner's documents give the register command queue one sentence
(only that the engine supports a register configuration queue, UM V1.00 p.663, 14.3). The head
layout and the RCQ_CTL / GLB_STS bits are the vendor driver's
(allwinner-bsp cubie-aiot-v1.4.8, de_base.h, de_top.c), a hint that
a test-only patch (drm-a733-de-rcq-probe, not carried) checked on silicon (6 GiB board,
2026-09-30, six requests): after RCQ_CTL bit 0 is set, the engine copies
each dirty head's block at TCON line 27/28 -- in the vertical blank, before
the first active line -- whatever line the request was made at (399, 616,
793, 1232); bit 0 clears itself and GLB_STS RCQ_FINISH sets within 2 us of
the register effect (bit 0 up to one poll earlier); writing GLB_STS back
clears FINISH; the head's dirty word is left to software; nothing is copied
again without a request; GLB_STS bit 4 read set at 399, 616, 793 and clear
at 1232/1233; GLB_STS[31:16] counts lines 33 behind the TCON. The probe as
rebased by this patch (6.18.54-trcq2, six runs) places the copy: bit 0
clears and the register takes the new value in the poll in which bit 4
rises or up to one poll (~2 us) before it, with the line field at 0, and
FINISH is set then or one poll later. After a display off, a request that
never completed left GLB_STS reading 0x02420110 (bit 4 set, line 578) 3
frames later -- a TCON stop leaving the engine frozen mid-frame (inferred).

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES

- A second commit backend behind begin/stage/arm, the default; module
  parameter sun60i_de.rcq (bool, 0644, read at each enable) = 0 selects
  the CPU stores as before, for A/B.
- 24 blocks, each head 16 bytes in one coherent page, shadows 32-byte
  aligned: blender attributes (0x60) and route..mode (0x24); VI0 and VI1
  layer 0 (0x30) and OVL_SIZE; VI0's CCSC, per instance the control word
  and the 3 x 4 matrix; VI0's VSU_CTL and VSU_OUT_SIZE. Every offset the
  driver stages is in one; one that is not warns once and is stored.
  So CCSC and VSU go through the queue like the rest.
- stage() writes a CPU copy; arm() waits for GLB_STS busy with a line
  field of at least 1 -- past the engine's first line -- and a status word
  that moves within two lines (a frozen engine does not; at most twice the
  vertical blank, vtotal - vdisplay lines, in all), then with interrupts off
  copies the marked blocks into their shadows, sets their dirty words and
  -- after clearing FINISH -- sets RCQ_CTL bit 0 (read-modify-write). A
  request still pending from earlier in the frame takes the new values
  along: commits coalesce, the last one wins, arm() never waits for a copy.
  If the pending request completed while the shadows were written, the
  request is made again; and once bit 0 has cleared, nothing is written
  until that request's FINISH has been seen (at most two lines' wait), so
  no write meets a copy still running. An engine that is not scanning (the DE comes up
  before the TCON and goes down after it) has its blocks written by CPU
  store.
- Completion is GLB_STS RCQ_FINISH set, or a newer request made (one is
  made only after the previous one's bit 0 cleared, and FINISH is cleared
  right before each). The CRTC's .atomic_flush waits for it
  (sun60i_de_kms_commit_wait(), sleeping, without the DE lock) and sends
  the event right after, so a flip completes when the engine has taken it.
  The copy follows the vblank interrupt in the same blank (interrupt at
  TCON line 7-9 in 282 of 300 samples, all 300 between line 1232 and 11,
  none after the copy's 27; measured with a script on the board), so
  the event carries the vblank count and timestamp of the frame that shows
  the commit; a vblank reference is held across the send. A legacy cursor
  update does not wait.
- A cursor that changes buffer or goes off, the primary layer going off,
  stop, and start's read-back wait for the copy under the lock -- the
  paths after which a buffer may be freed or a register is read back.
- A request not taken within three frames is withdrawn (RCQ_CTL bit 0
  cleared) and its dirty blocks are written by CPU store, with a log line.
  Every CPU store of blocks runs last block to last word first, so each
  enable word (layer ATTCTL, CCSC_CTL, PIPE_CTL) lands after what it
  enables.
- DE_BLD_INSTANCES 4 -> 1: the 0x400 steps are aliases of one register set
  (a write at 0x281400 + x reads back at 0x281000 + x and 0x281800 + x,
  0x282000 reads 0), and the extra writes would be a
  second, unqueued path to the shadowed registers.
- debugfs `state` names the backend, dumps GLB_STS, RCQ_CTL and the head
  registers, and counts requests, coalesced, direct, late, stale and
  timeouts.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES, AND WHAT WE DO INSTEAD (read for this patch, no code
taken; allwinner-bsp cubie-aiot-v1.4.8, drivers/drm/):

- Its shadows start at zero (bump allocator over dma_alloc_coherent, no
  read-back), so a dirty block sends every register it never wrote as 0.
  Here shadows are seeded from the registers at each enable.
- de_top_set_rcq_update() requests only while GLB_STS busy is set, spins
  up to 10000 x usleep_range(5, 10) for it, and returns 0 without having
  requested when it never comes; the caller learns of it only from the
  FINISH timeout. Here busy comes within one vertical blank on a scanning
  engine, and an engine that is not scanning is written directly.
- sunxi_de_atomic_flush() polls FINISH every 2 ms for up to 50 ms on every
  commit, cursor moves included, so a pointer move can block for a frame.
  Here only commits that carry an event or free a buffer wait.
- After FINISH it clears the dirty word of every block
  (de_rtmx_set_all_reg_dirty(hwde, 0)); a block made dirty between the
  request and the clear loses its flag. Here dirty words are written only
  inside the write window, and a request that completed meanwhile is made
  again.
- On a timeout it leaves the blocks dirty and sends the flip event anyway
  (sunxi_crtc_atomic_flush() -> sunxi_crtc_finish_page_flip()). Here the
  request is withdrawn and its blocks stored before the event goes.
- Its pad head for the 2-alignment is written one past the end of the head
  array (sunxi_de.c:363-366). Here the head count is even by construction.
- Its rtmx_start writes the blender output attributes by MMIO beside the
  queue so the TCON never sees a DE without output (sunxi_de.c:852-858).
  Here the engine is enabled before the TCON, so the whole first commit is
  a CPU store anyway, and start waits for a queued copy when it is not.

VERIFIED

Applies at --fuzz=0 on the full series (6.18.54); compile-check with and
without A733_TEST_KERNEL=1; sun60i-de.o and sun60i-kms.o W=1 clean.

On silicon, 6 GiB board, 2026-09-30, 6.18.54-trcq2 (this
patch before the write window moved from "busy" to "busy, line >= 1, status
moving"), sink CRC:
- a733-drm-cursor-stress, X stopped: still 1 CRC; jump at 125 Hz 2 and at
  1000 Hz 2 (before: 49 and 170); page flips at 60 Hz 2 CRCs, 703 of 703
  flips completed at 58.6 Hz.
- X pointer through XTest at ~110 Hz between two heights over the
  greeter's plain background: 2 and 2 CRCs; with sun60i_de.rcq=0 in the
  same boot (display off/on to re-enable): 45 and 34. Over the login box,
  where the pointer changes shape between the two heights (COORD1 x 949
  vs 957: another hotspot) and Xorg rewrites the cursor image in the same
  buffer: 7 and 6 (rcq=0: 45; default kernel before: 39 and 51). The same
  few extra CRCs recur in both runs, which fits the intermediate states of
  Xorg's image-then-move sequence rather than a torn frame (inferred).
- debugfs state: late 1; stale and timeouts 1 per display off, each a
  request made into the frozen engine. The version here closes that, and
  adds the FINISH guard a second review asked for.

This version on silicon, 6.18.54-trcq3, same board and day:
- cursor stress still 1, jump 125 Hz 2, jump 1000 Hz 2; page flips 2 CRCs,
  700 of 700 completed at 58.3 Hz;
- X pointer over the plain background 2 and 2 (rcq=0 in the same boot:
  28);
- two display off/on and a lightdm restart: late, stale, timeouts,
  nofinish all 0; no rcq line in journalctl -k -b.
- A shape change still corrupts frames while the client's cursor buffer is
  scanned (center of the greeter 6 and 7; in-place rewrite 17) -- that is
  drm-a733-the-cursor-scans-a-copy-of-its-image, the next patch.

OBSERVED BY EYE

The pointer moved fast over the desktop menu without flicker, by eye.

Assisted-by: Claude

